{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CghAhuwL8yXVHJtrNov3S", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Two services call PaymentsService's API directly: OrderService and RenewalsWorker.** Neither has any other payment route recorded in the graph, so both would lose the ability to take payments.\n\nPaymentsService exposes **PaymentsAPI**. The graph treats a service and the API it exposes as one place, so \"calling its API directly\" means one hop on a CALLS link to PaymentsAPI.\n\n| Service | Direct link | Its other dependencies (none handle payments) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **OrderService** | OrderService \u2192 CALLS \u2192 PaymentsAPI | Stores data in **OrdersPostgresDatabase**; depends on **UserService** |\n| **RenewalsWorker** | RenewalsWorker \u2192 CALLS \u2192 PaymentsAPI | Depends on **BillingService** |\n\n**Configuration:**\n- **AUTH_HOLD_MINUTES** configures RenewalsWorker. It sets how long a renewal authorization is held before capture. Renewal authorizations already on hold when PaymentsService goes down may not get captured within that window.\n- The graph has no configuration keys recorded for OrderService.\n\n**What the graph can't tell you:**\n- It shows call relationships, not runtime behaviour. \"Can no longer take payments\" assumes neither service has a fallback outside the graph, such as a retry queue or a second payment provider. None is recorded.\n- I only looked one hop out (direct callers), as the question asked. Services that depend on OrderService or RenewalsWorker would be hit indirectly, and I didn't include them."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 845, "cache_read_input_tokens": 4686, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 845}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": 